by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-13T16:28:59Z

Thanks for this, @maxbaldanza, and thanks for your patience. You flagged the overlap with #63422 yourself and rebased onto 8.2 unprompted, both appreciated.

The feature is worth having and the shape is right. The bridge already writes system attributes on the send side, it sets `AWSTraceHeader`, but had no way to read any back, so this closes an asymmetry rather than adding a new concept. The three calls you made are the ones I would have made:

 * extending `AmazonSqsReceivedStamp` instead of adding a second stamp, as #63422 did. It is already the "this came from SQS" carrier, and the new constructor argument is optional and trailing, so subclasses calling `parent::__construct($id)` are unaffected.
 * passing the raw AWS keys through. Normalising them would invent a second vocabulary for a class that is AWS-specific by name.
 * only adding `MessageSystemAttributeNames` to the request when the option is non-empty, so nothing changes for anyone who does not opt in.

Two things before this can go in:

 * The values are not validated. The connection carefully rejects unknown option *names*, `Unknown option found: [...]. Allowed options are [...]`, but any string can go into `message_system_attribute_names` and the user finds out from an opaque AWS error at receive time. Please check them against `MessageSystemAttributeName` in `fromDsn()` and fail there.
 * I am not convinced by the `[]` default. Requesting attributes is the same API call with a marginally larger response, there is no per-attribute charge, so asking for the useful ones by default costs close to nothing, while `[]` means `getSystemAttributes()` is empty for everyone who never discovers the option. The enum also offers `All`. Keep the option for people who want to trim the list, but consider a non-empty default.

One connection worth knowing about: #64569 proposes synthesising a `RedeliveryStamp` from broker-side redelivery counts. That is complementary rather than competing, and `ApproximateReceiveCount` is exactly what it would need on the SQS side. Whatever we decide there, this stays the lower-level primitive.

